<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/islam">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Islam (~1.9 billion, the fastest-growing major religion) centres on <strong>submission to
    God (the meaning of "islam") through the Quran, the Prophet Muhammad's example, and the five
    pillars of practice</strong>. Its history runs from 7th-century Arabia through a golden age
    of scholarship (the history lesson covers it) to global diversity — with the Sunni–Shia
    succession split as its deepest internal fault line.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="origins, pillars, the succession split, and the law" widget="equationStep" config={islamCfg} />

  <h2>What the study emphasises</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Quran as recitation:</strong> it is experienced aurally — "Quran" means recitation, and memorisation (hafiz) is a living tradition. The Arabic text is considered inimitable; translations are "interpretations".</li>
    <li><strong>Sufism: the mystical current:</strong> Rumi, Rabia, al-Ghazali — inward purification and love of God, sometimes in tension with legalism, often coexisting. Sufi orders carried Islam into West Africa, Southeast Asia and South Asia.</li>
    <li><strong>Islamic philosophy and science:</strong> Avicenna, Averroes, al-Ghazali's "Incoherence" debates — the tradition that preserved and extended Greek thought (the history lesson's golden age) while theologians argued about Greek logic's limits.</li>
    <li><strong>Modern currents are plural:</strong> from Ottoman modernism and Islamic modernism to Salafism, Islamism and secular Islam — a 1,400-year tradition interpreted through every modern political vocabulary, which is why "Islam says" is always the start of an argument, not the end.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the Quran (trans. Abdel Haleem); Esposito, Islam: The Straight Path; Lapidus, A History of Islamic Societies.</p>
</TopicPage>
